5. Middle-dot separator
   $ python3 -c '... find "Monthly Living Expenses setting" in handlers_spending_optimizer.go, print any char > U+007F'
   0xb7 '·'   (single codepoint, U+00B7 MIDDLE DOT, literal UTF-8 byte in
   the Go string literal, not an HTML entity)
   Rendered output confirms the literal character passes through
   html/template unescaped (html/template only escapes &<>"'): e.g.
   "...setting $7,639.34/mo · starting living budget $9,639.34/mo...". A
   bare "·" in a text node is either read as "middle dot" or silently
   skipped by screen readers — harmless noise, not suppressed/injected
   content (ACCESSIBILITY.md hidden-text-abuse check). Dropping it still
   reads sensibly: "...setting $7,639.34/mo starting living budget
   $9,639.34/mo (scheduled phases...)" remains comprehensible, and "·" as
   an inline separator is an established pattern already used elsewhere in
   this same partial at master (e.g. "0.0% · 0 of 1,000 futures",
   "0.0% · 0 / 1,000" in the frontier table), so this is not a new idiom.

